# Testing

The project uses three layers of tests. All of them run without network access.

## Layers

- **E2E (BDD)**: `cucumber` crate. One `.feature` file per screen under
  `tests/e2e/features/`. Step definitions under `tests/e2e/steps/`. A `World`
  struct under `tests/e2e/world.rs` holds stubbed ports and the in-memory screen
  stack. Scenarios walk the application the way a user would.
- **Functional (TDD)**: per-use-case tests under `tests/functional/`. Use
  `rstest` for parameterized cases. Each use case has at least one happy path
  and one failure path before merge.
- **Unit**: domain types and pure helpers. Plain `#[test]`. No mocks.

## Stubs and fixtures

- Every outbound port has a stub implementation under `tests/support/stubs.rs`
  (`StubTxReader`, `StubGasOracle`, ...). Stubs hold a map from input to
  fixture file name and return deserialized fixtures.
- Fixtures live under `tests/fixtures/` and are named
  `{adapter}__{method}__{case}.json`. Example:
  `rpc__eth_getTransactionByHash__usdc_transfer.json`.
- A fixture corresponds to exactly one scenario. Do not share fixtures across
  scenarios: copy and adjust instead.
- Fixture loader is `tests/support/fixture_loader.rs::load_json(path: &str)`.

## Rules

- No test makes a real HTTP or WebSocket call. CI runs with network access
  disabled; violations must fail the build loudly.
- Every public function gets a red test before any production code is written.
  "Red" here means the compiler forces you to implement the thing.
- Every BDD scenario is runnable in isolation. A failing scenario points at the
  exact step line.
- Every use case has at least one `Scenario:` in its `.feature` file; if a
  behaviour is not described by a scenario, it is not part of the product.
- Assertions use `assert_matches!` for enum shapes and `pretty_assertions`
  (`assert_eq!`) for structured data. Avoid bare `assert!(a == b)`.
- Time is injected. Never call `SystemTime::now()` inside application code; use
  a `Clock` port. Tests pass a `FrozenClock` stub.
- Randomness is injected. Never call the OS RNG directly; use an `Rng` port.
  Tests pass a seeded stub.

## Test naming

- Functional tests: `tests/functional/{use_case}.rs`, test name
  `it_{behaviour}_when_{precondition}`.
- Feature files: `tests/e2e/features/{screen}.feature`.
- Step definitions: `tests/e2e/steps/{screen}.rs`.

## Coverage gate

- Every use case must have a happy path and a failure path before its
  implementation is merged.
- Every adapter method must have a fixture for a happy response and at least
  one error response (for example a 429 and a 500 case).
- Every screen must have a "not found / empty state" scenario.

## Running

- `cargo test` runs unit + functional.
- `cargo test --test e2e` runs BDD scenarios.
- `cargo deny check` enforces dependency rules.
